which console, and how to tell
==============================

There is more than one way for a character to leave a Decision, and
until now they all arrived in the same window looking identical.  This
is what each of them is, which one is in use, and how that was measured
rather than assumed.


the two transmitters
--------------------

The MULTIO is the machine's console.  Three 8250s, and uart 0 is the one
Micronix opens: the kernel's tty driver for it is _mstty, and a boot that
reaches multi-user has used it for every character.

The DJ/DMA has a console of its own.  The controller is a Z80 with its
own firmware and its own bit banged serial port, reached by two channel
commands rather than by any port the CPU can address:

    2b  serial output       send the byte at channel+1
    2c  serial input enable  arm a poll that drops a received byte at
                             physical 3e and sets a flag at 3f

That port exists so a Decision with no serial board at all still has
somewhere to talk - the floppy controller is the only intelligent thing
in the box that has a UART.  A machine with a MULTIO has no reason to use
it, and a configuration that does is one built for a machine that did not
have one.  It is not a recommended configuration and nothing in this tree
is set up that way - but disks that are exist, and the archive's disk list
names a run of them ("DJDMA as console", "DJDMA serial console", around
disk 900).


which console a disk wants, and why it cannot be detected
---------------------------------------------------------

It is assembled in.  extra/hardware/.../floppy/930/CBIOS&.ASM selects a
console driver at assembly time with CONTYP, and CONTYP 4 is the DJ/DMA
one.  There is no byte on the disk that says so, no signature, and no
sane place to look for one: the same CP/M, the same everything else, and
a different console driver linked into the bios.

So there is nothing to detect up front.  What there is instead is the
moment of use, and it is unambiguous.  CONTYP 4 is short enough to quote:

    conout: lxi  h,dmchan
            mvi  m,serout       ; 2b
            inx  h
            mov  m,c
            jmp  docmd

    conin:  lxi  h,serin+1      ; poll 3f until the controller
            xra  a              ; deposits 40 there
    ci2:    cmp  m
            jz   ci2
            ...

    ttyset: ...
    dmaci:  db  writem          ; zot the monitor disable flag
            ...
            db  senabl          ; 2c - enable serial input
            db  1

Output is a channel command every time.  Input is not - conin and conist
only poll memory at 3e and 3f - but the poll has to be armed, and ttyset
arms it with senabl once at initialization.  Both ends therefore announce
themselves with a channel command, which is exactly what the controller's
own driver already decodes.  Hence the hook: no configuration switch, no
image sniffing, no guessing.  The bios says what it wants by doing it.

One aside for anyone booting such a disk here: ttyset also writes a byte
to 13f5 in controller memory - the comment calls it the "magical place in
monitor", zeroing a flag that would otherwise let the DJ/DMA's own
monitor have the serial port.  Our write_djmem only models the drive
parameter block at 1340, so it says "write_djmem 0x13f5 outside of
DPARAM" and ignores it.  That is correct behaviour and not a fault: we do
not simulate the controller's monitor, so there is nothing to disable.


why it was ambiguous
--------------------

terminal_fd_in and terminal_fd_out are defined by the serial board and
set to uart 0's descriptors (multio.c, at the end of multio_init).  The
DJ/DMA's serout wrote to terminal_fd_out and its input poll read from
terminal_fd_in, so:

    a character sent by the controller and a character sent by the
    MULTIO arrived in the same window, in the same order, with nothing
    to tell them apart

and, worse in the other direction:

    once the input poll is armed, a keystroke goes to whichever of the
    two asks for it first, and the other never sees it

Neither of those announced itself.  The output case is a reporting
problem - you cannot attribute what you are looking at - and the input
case is a real hazard, because a character eaten by the controller is
simply gone and the tty waits forever for it.


what is actually in use
-----------------------

Measured, booting the 1.67 standalone off the 5 1/4 floppy with the hard
disk attached - disks/hdinstall/run - through the loader's banner and
directory listing, the kernel's banner, and the whole of the welcome
text:

    DJ/DMA serial out      0 characters
    DJ/DMA serial in       0 characters, never armed
    MULTIO uart 0        773 characters, every one of them traced

So the second level loader does not use the controller's serial port
either, which is worth saying because it is the natural guess: it is a
standalone program talking to a machine it has just been read into by the
DJ/DMA, and it prints "Micronix DJDMA loader" as its first line.  It
still talks to the MULTIO.

The rule that follows: on this machine, console output is MULTIO output.
If something appears in the window that no txb trace accounts for, it did
not come from the machine.


the hook
--------

Ownership is a single variable, console_owner, and it starts at
CONS_MULTIO because that is the machine this tree simulates.  A device
claims it by using it:

    djdma serout (2b)       claims - conout does this per character
    djdma senabl (2c) with 1 claims - ttyset does this once
    multio uart 0 transmit  claims back

and ownership decides one thing only: who may take a character out of
the shared descriptor.  When the controller owns it, multio_uart_poll
leaves uart 0's input alone; when the multio owns it, djdma_poll_func
does.  Uarts 1 and 2 have descriptors of their own and are never in
question.

Output is deliberately not arbitrated.  Both devices keep writing to the
same window, because that is what somebody watching the machine sees, and
the traces now say which device each character came from.

A change of owner reports on stderr without a trace flag:

    console: input follows the djdma bit banged serial

Claiming what is already owned is silent, so the ordinary boot says
nothing.  Counters djserial_out and djserial_in hold the controller's
totals.

Last claim wins rather than first, so a machine whose monitor talks to
the multio and whose bios then switches to the controller ends up with
the console in the right place, and so does the reverse.  Nothing in the
tree exercises that yet.


what is and is not tested
-------------------------

The arbitration is verified in the direction that matters here: the
hdinstall boot claims nothing, stays on the multio, and is byte for byte
what it was before the change.

The controller-console direction is verified against the bios source
above and not yet by running one, because no CONTYP 4 disk is in the
tree.  Booting one is the test, and what it should print is the one
console: line, before its first character of output.

